WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
LORRAINE CINK: We're about to
show you how Captain America

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.130
faced off against
Captain America in Marvel

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.440
Studio's Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.980
Hey, I'm the stars
and the stripes.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:09.780
I'm Lorraine.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.190
 If you throw me,
I'll come right back.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.060
I'm Langston.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.740
This is Earth's Mightiest Show.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.110
 Hey, later on, musician
Ingrid Michaelson's going to pop

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.280
in to talk about her fandom.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.920
But first, here's our own
Agent M with the scoop

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.250
on Cap versus Cap.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.840
 Ooh, that's really
"America's Ask."

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.114
- Oh.
- I said ask.

00:00:24.114 --> 00:00:24.914
 Ah.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.350
 Hey, Marvel fans.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.840
I'm Ryan Penagos,
aka Agent M, and I

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.180
am so excited to be
sitting here with VFX

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.290
supervisor of Marvel Studios'
Avengers: Endgame, Dan DeLeeuw.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.090
How are you doing, Dan?

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:43.980
 Doing great.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.070
 So there's so much to
talk about in this film.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.280
You worked three years on it.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.547
Let's just dive right
into some stuff.

00:00:51.547 --> 00:00:52.347
Are you ready?

00:00:52.347 --> 00:00:53.440
 Yeah, let's go.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.320
RYAN PENAGOS: The first
thing I want to dig in to Dan

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.880
is Cap versus Cap.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.280
I don't believe you
guys have the technology

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.190
to clone Chris Evans yet.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:01.990
 Not yet.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.050
 But how did we bring
this battle to life?

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.640
How did you have to
Captain America's

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.100
fighting so intensely?

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.590
 A lot of the ways we shoot
the fights is that you'll have--

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.060
you'll get out there and shoot
it with two stunt performers,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.810
and then kind of run
the fight with them.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.610
And then you'll
bring Chris Evans in,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.680
and you'll shoot his
side of the fight.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.440
But in this case,
he shot both sides.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.190
And so the same thing--
we shot it with two stunt

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:27.000
doubles, and then Chris would
come in and play his side

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.620
and play the important side.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.110
And so from that you could kind
of get the different coverage

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.280
with the stunties
and the actors,

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.890
and then you get that, you know,
great acting with them as they

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:36.690
fight.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.390
You know, originally, we
shot it without the cowl,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.700
without the helmet
of the old suit.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.340
But then when we started
testing the movie,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.290
people were losing track
of which cap was which.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.250
Because we had the little--
the cut on his face

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.040
that he would have gotten
in the Battle of New York,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.080
but people weren't
following that,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.213
so ended up actually
having to put

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:57.540
a CG helmet onto Avengers Cap.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.580
What we did is we
worked with Lola.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.110
It's a visual effects
company, and they have

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.200
this thing we call the egg.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.200
You know, maybe the actor will
come in-- but Chris will come

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.650
in and you'll have three cameras
kind of from the general angle

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.610
in which you shot it.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.009
And then from that footage,
you'll take the three cameras,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.740
take the best angle,
build the best angle,

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.970
and then basically take
Chris Evans' head plus face

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.560
and put it on the stunt double.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.200
And the trick is that,
you know, a lot of times,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.050
you can't just stretch
the actor's face to match

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:25.950
the stunt double's head.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.280
So you end up having to like
warp the stunt person's head--

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.140
--and that's Chris Evans's head,
and then put his face on it.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:33.940
 Oh my gosh.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.040
 Yeah, so easy.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:36.840
 Sure.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:37.640
 Yeah.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.580
 Yeah, easy.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:44.130
So we're looking at this
scene, and all of it

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:49.770
essentially is created in
VFX, which blew me away

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.150
because when I watched
it, I was like, oh,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.810
that's a really cool set.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.140
So much of it seemed
practical and seemed so real,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.350
to the point where there's,
like, all this glass and all

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.060
this action that's going on.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.990
How much do you have to do
to bridge all those gaps?

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.900
And especially, the glass
and those little particles

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.470
seem wildly intricate
and difficult.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.660
 We knew when we shot it,
it was going to be glass.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.150
All the artwork that
everyone was attracted to

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:23.190
was this kind of big,
open-space glass skyscraper

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.860
for portraying the
insides of Stark Tower.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.350
And it's something that we
couldn't find practically

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.760
and we couldn't
necessarily build.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.070
So basically,
everything you see,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.460
we basically modeled
inside of Stark Tower.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.190
You've got one bridge that
we built made of glass that

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.300
was reflecting green screen.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.820
So pretty much, that was great
to have, but then that got

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.680
replaced quite a lot too
because you couldn't pull

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.660
the mat off the green screen.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.000
But all the offices you see on
the side are dressed, you know,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.530
like you would dress a set.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.390
You know, you build all
the office equipment

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.310
you could possibly want and
start dressing everything

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.380
from the sides.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.390
And it was interesting
because the set

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:02.190
that we built digitally was
incredibly detailed-- we

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.690
found that we were cutting it.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.480
There was so much
complexity to it,

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.630
that on the shortcuts of how
we usually share our fights,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.910
you couldn't follow the action
anymore because you needed

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:16.110
to kind of get the clean
silhouette of Cap fighting Cap

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.140
with all that busy background.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.140
So we ended up having to go in
and just adding a little more

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.720
atmosphere, kind of cheating
the depth of field a little bit

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.550
just to kind of pull
some of the detail

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.660
out that we had put
into it so there

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.070
wasn't as much visual
information going

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:27.870
into your eye.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.710
So it was a roundabout
way to get there.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:30.510
 Sure.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.300
 But, you know, it
ended up working.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.050
RYAN PENAGOS: In Stark Tower,
you know, so much of it is VFX,

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.460
but there's also a practical
set of stairs that was actually

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.760
built. How do you and
the VFX team, you know,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.430
work with the practical
side of things

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.040
to make sure that
all comes together?

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.450
 Well, essentially,
I like shooting

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.630
as much practically as we can.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.710
And it's, you know, we have
this relationship with the stunt

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.840
team, and Cap falling
and smacking things

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.700
seems to be one of
their favorite things.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.415
You kind of-- you let the
stunties do their craft.

00:05:02.415 --> 00:05:04.440
You keep them safe,
pan out whatever

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.290
pads they need, and then, you
know-- but let them, you know,

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:08.130
do their wire work.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.290
And you get that-- you
know, you kind of get just

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.120
to that edge of
something that feels

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.490
like it's really going to
hurt, but Cap can survive it.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.110
And so when you look at
the scene, you've got,

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.580
you know, the bridge.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.500
The stunties fall
off the bridge.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:20.340
Then you cut wide.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.410
You go to CG digi
doubles of Cap.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:23.910
And then the shots
looking down where they're

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.650
bashing between the
two stairs, and you're

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.590
back to stunties again, and then
you're panning out the wires.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.070
So you let them do it, you know?
RYAN PENAGOS: Yeah.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.037
DAN DELEEUW: And then that's
what makes it look real, makes

00:05:32.037 --> 00:05:33.550
it look feel like it hurts.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.350
 It definitely
looks like it hurts.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:36.433
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:38.500
DAN DELEEUW: You know, it's kind
of a love letter to the fans.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.790
But it's kind of a
love letter for us

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.890
too, just because we had
worked on so many films,

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.530
and especially with
Cap, so that kind of--

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.040
you know, when you go
into it, it's like, OK,

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:46.840
what has to happen?

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.305
And it's like, well, you have
to have the shield throw.

00:05:48.305 --> 00:05:50.530
You know, you've got to get a
shield throw in there somehow.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.690
You know, it's a lot of the--
your love of the characters.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.490
 I felt there was also love
for that American tuchus

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.010
that we saw--
DAN DELEEUW: There is.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:57.580
RYAN PENAGOS: --right
at the end there.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:58.200
DAN DELEEUW: It's
the tuchus, yes.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.000
[LAUGHS]

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.940
 Dan, thanks so much
for sharing so much

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:03.130
about Marvel Studios'
Avengers: Endgame.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.015
You guys, make sure you
pick up the film now.

00:06:06.015 --> 00:06:08.925
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.490
 It is mind-blowing
the work that

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.890
goes into this from
all the fight choreo

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.850
to just building the world.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:18.857
- Yeah.
- It's nuts.

00:06:18.857 --> 00:06:20.090
 Yeah, you mention
the fight choreo.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:21.630
Learning two sides
of the same fight

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.853
so you can fight yourself, I
mean, it sounds like a blast.

00:06:23.853 --> 00:06:25.520
It sounds like my dream,
but it sounds like a lot.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:26.740
- Would you fight yourself?
- Oh, absolutely.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:27.560
 Yeah, same.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:28.660
I would fight
myself in a minute.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:29.870
 In a heartbeat, yeah.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.580
Well, listen, you don't
have to fight yourself

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.190
when you get Marvel
Studios' Avengers: Endgame

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.930
on digital and Blu-ray now.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:36.830
LORRAINE CINK: Oh, my gosh.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.750
I can't wait to watch
all those deleted scenes

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.300
and bloopers because
that's my favorite part.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:40.610
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:41.943
Well, hey, in the
comics Captain America

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:43.440
has fought himself
before, you know?

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.430
 Hey, that's true.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:45.230
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:49.190
 It was actually a
reality splintered evil

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.887
version of himself in the
SECRET EMPIRE comic book event.

00:06:51.887 --> 00:06:53.710
LANGSTON BELTON: Yeah, yeah.
LORRAINE CINK: He was not nice.

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.220
LANGSTON BELTON: Evil
is putting it mildly.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:56.287
LORRAINE CINK: Yeah, he was bad.

00:06:56.287 --> 00:06:58.340
LANGSTON BELTON: He was quite,
quite an evil person, yes.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.140
 Yeah.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.775
 Well, hey, everyone's
a fan of Captain America,

00:07:00.775 --> 00:07:02.375
so the more the merrier--
just not the bad ones,

00:07:02.375 --> 00:07:03.175
pretty please.

00:07:03.175 --> 00:07:05.630
 Yeah, just the good ones, like
in Marvel Studios' Avengers:

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.300
Endgame-- all right, our next
guest, Ingrid Michaelson,

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.880
is actually taking her
fandom to the next level with

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.580
her new album, Stranger Songs.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.560
 Check it out.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.820
 I am here with the incredible
musician Ingrid Michaelson.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:19.620
Hello.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.420
 Hi.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.760
 I'm so excited to talk to you.

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.180
Especially because
I've heard that you--

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:27.180
you have some Marvel
fandom going on.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:27.980
 I do.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.290
My boyfriend's daughter and
her boyfriend our obsessed

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:32.450
with Marvel, so I had
a Spider Gwen cake

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.600
made for her birthday, and
I specifically designed

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.580
it myself based on her colors--

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.650
the pink, turquoise,
blue, and white--

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.700
and had a little Spider
Gwen cake topper.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.000
 So you've also been
pulled into this.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.500
 I didn't make the cake.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.810
I designed the cake.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:47.610
 But that counts.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:48.470
 And then--

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:49.337
 You were cake architect.

00:07:49.337 --> 00:07:52.760
 --I picked it up and bought
it-- yes, yes, cake architect.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.090
 But let's talk about
your new album, Stranger

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.340
Songs, which is incredible.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:01.850
I've been listening to it
weirdly on repeat at my desk.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.350
Where did the album come from?

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.090
I know you're a big
Stranger Things fan,

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:06.650
and clearly, it's
also into your family.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:07.450
 Yes.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.110
So I love the show.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:10.820
I love nostalgia.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:12.470
I love sci-fi.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:14.210
And I wanted to
do something that

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:17.270
was different and inspiring,
and this show specifically, it

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.070
was just one of those
things that I, like, allowed

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.660
myself to just get lost in.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:23.730
The way it makes me feel--
it reminds me of my youth.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.520
So I started writing
poems about the show.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.420
And then I was like, if I'm
writing poems about the show,

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.140
maybe I should write
a song about this.

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:31.880
And then I just
started performing

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:35.670
these songs from the point of
view of all the characters.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:36.770
But if you haven't
seen the show,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.120
you still can appreciate it.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.669
It's not, like, so
specific that you won't

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:42.289
get what the song's about.

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:44.660
But if you know the show, you're
like, oh, I know what that

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:45.680
is-- oh, I know what that is.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.113
There's like little
Easter eggs everywhere.

00:08:47.113 --> 00:08:49.610
 I especially love
the song, "Pretty,"

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.390
and I loved the sentiment
that you shared with it

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:54.590
about what that song was about.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:55.350
Would you talk a
little bit about it?

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:56.240
INGRID MICHAELSON: Yeah.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.640
So there's that scene
where they dress

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.030
11 up in a blonde wig and a
pink dress, and Mike's like,

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:03.683
oh, you look pretty
good-- you look

00:09:03.683 --> 00:09:05.150
pretty good because
he's embarrassed that he

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:05.950
thinks she looks pretty.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:09.470
But the idea of what makes a
girl or a woman pretty, what

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:13.820
makes a woman beautiful, it's
this sort of like blonde hair,

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.820
pink dress trope that I think
the Duffer brothers were

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.040
playing up on, which then,
of course, she throws

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:20.930
off basically, casts aside.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.300
And it was just the concept of
what makes a woman beautiful,

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.990
I thought, would be a really
interesting take on that scene

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:29.610
and how to make something
very anthemic and powerful

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:30.710
for people to listen to.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:33.350
 I found it so stirring
because there is something--

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.130
I think you even say,
fight like a girl--

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:35.930
INGRID MICHAELSON: Yeah.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:36.740
 --in the lyric.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.380
 She's the strongest
character in the whole show.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.190
 Yeah.

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:41.530
 We don't see that often--

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:44.590
a small girl is the
strongest character,

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:45.710
I mean, like, from
the beginning.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.260
Like, it's just really rad.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:51.680
 Well, because you seem
to have this affinity

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:55.250
for girl superheroes,
I thought, what if we

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:56.460
did a girl superhero quiz?

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.980
What do you think?
INGRID MICHAELSON: Yeah, yeah.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:58.580
LORRAINE CINK: Great.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:09:59.570
I'm going to give
you some questions.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:00.803
You're going to give
me your answers.

00:10:00.803 --> 00:10:02.030
We're all going to
be better for it.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:02.830
 [LAUGHS]

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.180
 All right, you
desperately need a snack.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:06.590
What do you grab?

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:11.300
A, nuts, of course; B,
dinosaur-shaped nuggets;

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:17.060
C, a slice of pizza; or D, a
plant-based superfood smoothie?

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:17.860
 A piece of pizza.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:18.660
 Oh, yes.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:19.820
Well, you're from New York, so.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:21.050
 Yeah, Staten Island.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.840
 Yeah, you know a
good pieces of za.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:23.640
 Za.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:26.650
 [LAUGHS] It's your day
off-- where do you go?

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:28.490
A, out with my big
group of friends?

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:29.290
INGRID MICHAELSON: No.

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:30.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.500
 B, I hide out
tinkering with a project?

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:37.010
C, I take a ride and
feel the wind in my hair?

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.790
Or D, I take time to meditate?

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:43.080
 2, tinkering around
with a project.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:43.880
 Excellent.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:45.200
 Or B, whatever it was.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.700
 B2 it is.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:47.960
Time to get dressed--

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:49.580
what is your style?

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:53.150
A, yoga pants are
comfy and fashionable;

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:57.860
B, overalls and glasses,
please; C, I love a jumpsuit

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:02.390
and aviators; or D,
something befitting royalty?

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.340
 Glasses and overalls
because literally that's

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:05.810
what I wear most of the time.

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.660
 Amazing, so B it is.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:10.700
Who is your best friend?

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:13.470
A, I have tons of best friends?

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:16.800
B, my pet and I are
basically the same person?

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.270
C, they're a mouthy dynamo and
my partner in crime fighting?

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:25.150
Or D, I'm more of a
lone wolf-- or panther?

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:27.930
 C. C, a mouthy dynamo--

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:28.920
I like it.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.470
If you were a song, you'd be--

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:37.080
A, my own hummable theme song;
B, mathematically perfect;

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.440
C, anything you can
bump on the radio;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.266
or D, the songs of my ancestors.

00:11:43.266 --> 00:11:45.390
 [LAUGHS] A.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.970
 Your own hummable theme song.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:48.930
I love it.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:50.280
All right, Ingrid,
it looks like you

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:52.290
got mostly Bs, which
means that you are

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:54.820
most like Lunella Lafayette.

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:56.100
 That's so cute.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.720
LORRAINE CINK: Not only are you
the smartest person on earth--

00:11:58.720 --> 00:11:59.940
INGRID MICHAELSON: Oh?
Mm-hmm.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.030
LORRAINE CINK: --you're
a kid at heart--

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:01.830
 Yeah.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:02.740
 --and maybe in real life.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:04.680
Luckily, you probably
have a giant dinosaur

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.950
also to carry you around since
you're not old enough to drive.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.080
 How young am I?

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:09.880
 8.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:10.740
 I love it.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:11.740
 So young.

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:12.690
 My skin is so young.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:13.850
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:14.820
 Well, well done.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.260
You're well on your
way to also being

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:18.870
a superhero, and
congratulations on all

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:20.280
of your amazing endeavors.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.120
Ingrid, thank you so
much for talking with us.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:23.160
 Thank you.
Thanks for having me.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:23.960
 Oh, of course.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.490
You guys, be sure to
listen to Strangers Songs

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:28.140
wherever you listen to
music, and check out

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.110
IngridMichaelson.com
for tour dates and more.

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:33.390
 Bye, thank you.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.760
 Hey, Lunella
Lafayette-- great choice,

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.300
great person to identify with.

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:39.210
I appreciate solitude
sometimes as well,

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.620
so it works out perfectly.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.830
 Yeah, it's great.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:44.490
We actually shot that interview
with Ingrid before the news

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.710
that David Harbour, who plays
Detective Hopper on Stranger

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.530
Things, will be in Marvel
Studios' Black Widow,

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.260
and I'm sure she would've
been so pumped to know.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:52.970
So now you know, Ingrid, enjoy.
- Yes.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:54.170
Oh, hey, actually,
Lorraine, you got

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:56.240
to talk to Mr. David Harbor--

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:57.040
 I did.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:58.140
 --at San Diego Comic-Con.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:12:58.940
So, hey, you know what?

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.390
Just for fun, let's
take a quick look

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.100
at David Harbour on the
press tour for Marvel

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:05.940
Studios' Black Widow at SDCC.

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:08.290
 What does it mean for you
to join the Marvel Universe?

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.690
You're no stranger to fandom.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:11.860
- What?
- As it were.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:13.160
 Look at what you did there.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.180
 Right, Stranger Things.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.160
What does it mean to you
to be part of the MCU?

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:19.650
 I mean, look,
these guys are like--

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:23.080
these guys are like the
best at what they do.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.170
So it's like, you know--

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:28.810
it's like delicious.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:29.910
I mean, I don't
know what to say.

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:30.990
It's wonderful.

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:32.880
Like it's-- the
scripts are so good.

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:34.170
The characters are so good.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.110
The directors-- Cate
Shortland is so good.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.570
The movies are all so
unique in and of themselves.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.060
Black Widow is, in
a way, a departure.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.040
I mean, it feels almost
like an espionage movie.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.140
It's got all these
elements to it.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.160
So it's like wonderful.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.800
I mean, I wish I
could say things that

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.810
were interesting in these
interviews, but all I can say

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:53.520
is like, it's great.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:54.510
And it is-- it's great.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:55.420
 For us, that is great.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.220
 OK.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:57.210
 Because we know nothing.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.590
 OK, OK.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:13:59.510
 Thank you so much.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.260
 Thank you.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.740
 Awesome-- awesome to see
David Harbour's passion already

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:05.970
coming out of his pores.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:06.770
Like, I can't wait--

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:07.800
I mean, it's so good.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:08.600
 Yeah.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.680
 I love when I see someone
excited to join the MCU,

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:11.660
so we can't wait.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.040
 Yea, yea, I can't wait
either to see Marvel Studios'

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:16.690
Black Widow in May of 2020--

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:17.490
May 1.

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:19.110
 Yeah, so anyway, we're going
to attempt to be patient.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:19.910
 [SIGHS]

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:20.747
 Deep breaths and--

00:14:20.747 --> 00:14:21.680
 Oh, it's going to be hard.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:22.525
 --a lot of--

00:14:22.525 --> 00:14:23.790
a lot of meditation.
[LAUGHS]

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:24.870
 Yeah, that's it.

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:26.700
All right, tell us what
your favorite fight

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.500
scene is in Marvel
Studios' Avengers: Endgame.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.200
And use that hashtag--
#earthsmightiestshow.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:33.780
 Tell us in the comments, and
we might just use your comment

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:34.620
in a future episode.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:35.990
But until then, we'll
see you next time.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:36.900
 Yeah, I'm Lorraine.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:37.700
 And I'm Langston.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:38.850
 And this is Marvel.

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:39.750
 Your universe.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:42.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

